WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.160
Private Equity consistently makes brands&nbsp;
worse, in the name of short-term profit.&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.680
But, what happens when it doesn’t work out?&nbsp;
What happens when a firm thinks they can turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.680
a business around… but everything collapses?
These are companies where Private Equity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:25.040
backfired, each one gets worse for the company and&nbsp;
in a few cases, disastrous for the investors too.&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.280
Starting with one of the most recent,&nbsp;
that you might have seen in headlines.&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.120
Red Lobster, the popular US restaurant chain, was&nbsp;
bought by Golden Gate Capital for $2.1 billion.&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.680
Except... Golden Gate had found a unique&nbsp;
way to cash in on one of Red Lobster’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.000
biggest assets.
Real estate.&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.400
When Golden Gate bought Red Lobster, to&nbsp;
complete the transaction, they sold off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.520
the real estate to another company.
Then… leased it back to Red Lobster.&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:01.200
This turned each location of Red Lobster&nbsp;
into an additional expense. By 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.800
the annual rent rose up to $190 million.
But where did all the money from selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.160
real estate go?
…to investors.&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.400
“Much of the liquidity from the sale-leaseback&nbsp;
went toward paying dividends to private equity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.520
investors rather than addressing systemic&nbsp;
operational issues or adapting the menu and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.480
brand to shifting market demands,”
This was, quite obviously, insane.&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.440
And all this was occurring at a critical time&nbsp;
where casual dining was already under pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.800
from weaker traffic.
And it gets worse.&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.200
After extracting value from Red Lobster,&nbsp;
Golden Gate started reducing its stake by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.160
selling 25% of the company to Thai&nbsp;
Union for $575 million in 2016.&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.680
In a few years, Golden Gate was gone, while&nbsp;
Red Lobster was someone else's problem.&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.880
But the new owners weren't much good either.
To get more traffic, Red Lobster turned its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.760
$20 Ultimate Endless Shrimp promotion from&nbsp;
a limited-time deal into a permanent deal.&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.280
Though it brought in more traffic, it led to a&nbsp;
loss of about $11 million in one quarter alone.&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:10.080
By May 2024, Red Lobster had filed for bankruptcy&nbsp;
after racking up nearly $300 million in debt.&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.520
Which revealed some other, strange occurrences.
Red Lobster's then-CEO Jonathan Tibus alleged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.240
that former CEO Paul Kenny had dropped two of&nbsp;
the company's breaded-shrimp suppliers under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.440
the cover of a "quality review," leaving Thai&nbsp;
Union with an exclusive deal at a higher cost.&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.000
The chain immediately closed at least&nbsp;
99 restaurants across 28 states.&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.240
It eventually emerged from bankruptcy&nbsp;
in September 2024 under new ownership.&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.360
Thai Union paid $575 million for its&nbsp;
slice of Red Lobster. In January 2024&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.760
it wrote off $530 million.
But Golden Gate Capital… who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.280
I’d argue are the real villains, they&nbsp;
got away with pretty much everything.&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:57.440
But private equity doesn't&nbsp;
always get away with it…

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.160
Getting through all these financials took a&nbsp;
lot of reading, fact-checking, and research.&nbsp;

