00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.080
By this point, Terra Firma, led by Guy Hands,&nbsp;
had already been involved in everything from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.480
buying out businesses like care homes to&nbsp;
the highly controversial privatisation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.120
thousands of military homes.
His next prize was EMI.&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.720
Guy Hands thought he had struck gold once again.
"for the worst business we can find in the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.160
challenged sector and we get really happy&nbsp;
if it's really, really bad. EMI, our most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:44.160
recent investment, is a classic example. We're&nbsp;
just hoping EMI is as bad as we think it is."&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.640
Very weird, but from Guy’s point of view,&nbsp;
the worse the business, the cheaper it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.400
and the bigger the return if you fix it.
So in 2007, Terra Firma acquired EMI for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.320
£4.2 billion but only £1.75 billion of&nbsp;
Terra firma’s money went into this deal.&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.360
Citigroup agreed to fund the rest, and EMI&nbsp;
ended up owing the bank “more than £3 billion.”&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.960
And weeks after the deal closed, the credit&nbsp;
markets seized up. Which meant Citigroup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.600
couldn't sell that debt on to other lenders.
Guy Hands had miscalculated what he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.280
dealing with: another big, growing problem.
Physical music sales were already declining.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.760
Piracy was rising, while digital downloads&nbsp;
were changing how people bought music.&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:30.000
EMI needed time to adapt.
Hands treated EMI like a cost problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.760
like he did many other industries.
Hands then announced around 2,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.680
jobs would be cut along with up to&nbsp;
£200 million in annual spending.&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.200
But, music labels are a bit different. EMI's&nbsp;
value was in its artists and music catalogue.&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.040
And artists aren’t really&nbsp;
“assets”. Artists can walk.&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.480
And this was already a big problem for EMI.&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.000
It had been losing its biggest&nbsp;
names even before Hands arrived.&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.560
Paul McCartney had left in 2007, and&nbsp;
Radiohead had left, years earlier.&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.800
EMI had been trying to re-sign them&nbsp;
since, but, after the takeover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.120
Radiohead was so frustrated with the new&nbsp;
management that they released their next album,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.920
In Rainbows, through their own website.
Then, after a meeting with Guy went so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.080
badly, Mick Jagger took the Rolling&nbsp;
Stones' catalogue to Universal Music.&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.320
"It's been taken over by somebody who's&nbsp;
never owned a record company before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.200
Guy Hands and Terra Firma, and they don't&nbsp;
realise what they're dealing with. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:30.000
really sad to leave all the people. Terra&nbsp;
Firma doesn't understand the music industry."&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.920
And Guy Hands wasn't ready to take the loss.
He sued Citigroup, arguing that the bank had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.840
"tricked him" into buying&nbsp;
EMI at an inflated price.&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.240
But he lost the case.
By 2011, the debt finally caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:48.640
up with Terra Firma, which had to absorb&nbsp;
the financial consequences of the deal.&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:54.560
So Citigroup took control of EMI.
Terra Firma's roughly £1.75 billion investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:00.800
was wiped out, while Citigroup wrote off another&nbsp;
£2.2 billion of EMI's debt before selling it off.&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.760
Universal took the recorded-music business, while&nbsp;
a Sony-led consortium took music publishing.&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:12.240
EMI was no longer the independent British music&nbsp;
giant it once was, all thanks to private equity.&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.480
Years later, another private equity firm&nbsp;
would take the same basic idea into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.520
food industry… which backfired terribly.
And this time, it would involve some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:30.960
of the biggest brands in America.
In 2013, 3G Capital teamed up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.000
Berkshire Hathaway to acquire Heinz. The ketchup&nbsp;
bottles many of you might use every single day.&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.280
Then, just two years later, they merged Heinz&nbsp;
with Kraft Foods Group, creating the world's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.880
fifth-largest food and beverage company.
But behind the scenes, the same strategy 3G&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.320
used to build it was beginning to tear it apart.
They went after every expense they could find.&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.600
If Kraft Heinz couldn't grow quickly, it could&nbsp;
still become more profitable by spending less.&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.880
Yet this came at an… inopportune time.
Consumers were moving toward fresher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.280
healthier, less processed products, while&nbsp;
private-label brands were getting better.&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.000
That meant Kraft Heinz needed to invest more&nbsp;
in innovation, marketing, and its brands.&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.960
But, 3G was focused on cutting costs.
And the cracks were starting to show.&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:22.880
In 2019, Kraft Heinz expected to make&nbsp;
between $6.3 and $6.5 billion in profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:28.160
before certain costs, well below the&nbsp;
$7.47 billion analysts had expected.&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:31.840
The company was also forced to cut its&nbsp;
quarterly dividend from around 63 cents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:38.320
to 40 cents per share. Investors, naturally,&nbsp;
weren’t too happy, but it gets much worse.&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:42.880
On February 21, 2019, the&nbsp;
company had to write down $15.4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:48.880
billion in goodwill and intangible assets,&nbsp;
including the Kraft and Oscar Mayer trademarks.&nbsp;

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:54.800
That turned into a $12.6 billion net loss.
Kraft Heinz also disclosed that the SEC was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.160
investigating its procurement&nbsp;
accounting, including how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.480
handled agreements with suppliers.
Investors didn't take the news well.&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.640
Kraft Heinz's stock plunged&nbsp;
more than 27% in a single day.&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.960
Kraft Heinz showed how aggressive&nbsp;
cost-cutting could weaken a company over time.&nbsp;

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:22.080
But what happens when private equity takes&nbsp;
that risk and adds billions of dollars in debt?&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.160
If you’ve watched this far, you&nbsp;
probably like our videos! So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.560
consider subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;
and means you won’t miss future videos.&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:38.000
In 2005, Bain Capital, KKR and Vornado Realty&nbsp;
Trust bought Toys “R” Us for $6.6 billion.&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.520
But they only put up about $1.3&nbsp;
billion of their own money.&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:47.280
About 80% of the deal was financed with debt.
And that debt would soon become a problem.&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:53.040
As more customers were moving online, Toys “R” Us&nbsp;
needed to invest heavily in its online business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:58.800
improve its stores, compete on prices, and offer&nbsp;
products customers couldn't easily find elsewhere.&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.800
Instead, a huge chunk of its cash&nbsp;
was going toward interest payments.&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.920
The company was reportedly spending about&nbsp;
$400 million a year just servicing its debt.&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.840
And the competition wasn't getting any easier.
Walmart had enormous buying power and low prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.880
Amazon had the convenience and variety of online&nbsp;
shopping, and even offered same-day delivery.&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:24.960
Toys “R” Us needed to adapt, but with so much&nbsp;
cash going toward debt, it was pretty much stuck.&nbsp;

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:28.320
Suppliers became more cautious about&nbsp;
doing business with the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.200
making it harder to secure enough inventory for&nbsp;
the more profitable seasons, like the holidays.&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:37.120
Toys “R” Us couldn't keep up.
In 2018, it announced plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:43.280
to close all 885 of its U.S. stores,&nbsp;
putting as many as 33,000 jobs at risk.&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:46.480
But there’s something particularly&nbsp;
sinister about the collapse.&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:52.080
Over the years, Bain Capital, KKR and&nbsp;
Vornado collected about $464 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:57.360
from Toys “R” Us through advisory fees,&nbsp;
transaction fees, expenses and interest on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.000
loans they had made to the company.
Toys “R” Us was being bled dry.&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.360
By June 29, 2018, every remaining&nbsp;
U.S. Toys “R” Us store was gone.&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.440
Pretty sad, to be honest.
But Toys “R” Us wasn't the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:16.560
retail giant struggling under financial pressure.
This last one, is one of the largest private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:25.360
equity disasters in history.
In 2005, Eddie Lampert pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:30.160
off an $11 billion deal that was supposed to&nbsp;
bring two struggling retailers back to life.&nbsp;

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:35.040
He merged Kmart with Sears, creating&nbsp;
one of the biggest retailers in America.&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:39.920
Lampert had a huge vision for this deal.
Kmart had a huge network of stores and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:45.040
valuable real estate, while Sears had well-known&nbsp;
brands in appliances, tools and home goods.&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:50.080
The plan was to sell Sears products in Kmart&nbsp;
stores. Craftsman tools, Kenmore appliances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.960
in front of a whole new set of customers.
Hundreds of Kmarts would be converted into Sears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:59.840
stores, moving the brand out of dying shopping&nbsp;
malls. They'd also merge the two head offices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:04.560
and buy stock for both chains at once.
Lampert promised it would save up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:08.240
$500 million a year.
And when Sears needed more cash, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:13.840
could sell off some of its many valuable brands.
For example, in 2017, Sears sold the Craftsman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:18.480
brand to Stanley Black &amp; Decker for $900 million.
It also raised money by selling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.320
borrowing against its real estate.
Sears sold hundreds of its properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.840
to Seritage Growth Properties, then leased many&nbsp;
of them back so the stores could keep operating.&nbsp;

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:32.560
But there was a problem.
Lampert was also chairman of Seritage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:36.320
while his investment firm, ESL, had&nbsp;
a significant stake in the company.&nbsp;

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:41.040
Some shareholders alleged that valuable Sears&nbsp;
assets were being sold too cheaply, benefiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:46.800
Lampert and ESL while leaving Sears weaker.
Another problem was... the money wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.000
being reinvested back into Sears stores.
As the retail industry changed, Walmart and Target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:57.520
competed on price and convenience, Home Depot and&nbsp;
Lowe's dominated home improvement, and of course…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:03.440
there was Amazon. Sears kept getting older, and&nbsp;
the company's e-commerce business lagged behind.&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:09.040
At the time of the 2005 merger, Sears and&nbsp;
Kmart had roughly 3,500 stores combined.&nbsp;

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:14.800
By the time Sears filed for bankruptcy in October&nbsp;
2018, that number had fallen to around 700.&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:19.520
The company listed about $6.9 billion&nbsp;
in assets but owed $11.3 billion.&nbsp;

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:23.440
Which brings us back to Lampert.
His investment firm, ESL, later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:27.120
bought many of Sears' remaining assets&nbsp;
through a company called Transformco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:32.320
keeping a smaller version of Sears alive.
But the Sears and Kmart that once had thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:36.640
of stores across America were gone.
After all these stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:40.480
one pattern keeps showing up.
Private equity can come in, load a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:46.160
with debt, cut costs, sell off valuable assets,&nbsp;
and sometimes, still walk away with a return.&nbsp;

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:51.600
But the company doesn't always recover.
Employees lose their jobs and stores disappear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:57.120
Products and customer experiences get worse.&nbsp;
And brands people love erode, or are bled dry.&nbsp;

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:02.080
So, it’s hard to blame so many people for&nbsp;
having such disdain, for private equity.&nbsp;

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:05.520
Another beloved brand was recently&nbsp;
acquired by Private Equity.&nbsp;

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.480
Southwest Airlines.
One of the most beloved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:13.920
but also, consistently profitable companies?
So, what would private equity change?&nbsp;

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:16.080
Watch this video next to&nbsp;
learn the rest of the story.

